PATCH 570: the MGAVIDEO clipped line drawer 1677 rebuilt 1:1 -- the clip block was 32-bit and never wrote
the clipped endpoint back.

fist_mga.c FUN_0000_1677 (asm 0x1677-0x1951) is the line primitive behind every HUD line: reticle,
graticule, the polyline records 5fb0 emits every frame.  Patch 136 rebased its bases and left the clip
"control flow as the pristine decompile (faithful)".  It was not: Ghidra emulated the `cmp ; lahf ;
rol ax,1` outcodes as sign tests over 32-bit `uint` differences of zero-extended words -- the asm's
outcode is the 16-bit SF of each `cmp` -- and its rendering of the 1722 clip step (imulw/idivw on
[bp-0xa]/[bp-0xc], written back through SI to the point being clipped) never reached the coordinates the
draw section reads.  First seen on AZER1 with 562-569: the frame after the player's last vehicle dies
(word[0x6d34]=0) projects a polyline point to x = 28759; the unclipped draw read rowtab[28759] far past
the 320-entry table and the pixel address left g_mem (SIGSEGV at 1677+... in m_mga 1677 <- 161c <- 38aa
<- 378e).  In the original the same line is clipped to the window edge -- it keeps rendering frames
through the 40-tick DEFEAT countdown with no vehicle at all.

Reconstruction, in 16-bit arithmetic throughout:
  * outcodes L<<3 | R<<2 | T<<1 | B from the 16-bit sign of x-xmin, xmax-x, y-ymin, ymax-y against the
    descriptor's window (desc = DGROUP:word[0x724]: [6]=xmin [8]=ymin [0xa]=xmax+1 [0xc]=ymax+1);
    shared-side reject; both-inside -> draw; the [bp-0x10] toggle gives ONE clipping pass, a second
    failure rejects;
  * the 1722 step per endpoint: L/R move x to the edge and y by ddy*(edge-x)/ddx, T/B move y to the
    edge and x by ddx*(edge-y)/ddy (T/B only when actually past the edge: `jge`/`jl`), imulw/idivw
    signed and truncating, written back, then the other endpoint (xchg cl,ch ; jcxz -> recompute);
  * draw: the MGA target is COLUMN-major -- pixel = fb + rowtab[x] + y, rowtab = word[DGROUP:0x794],
    one column = desc[4] bytes; vertical fill (rep stosw + stosb / the xor pair), horizontal fill (two
    pixels per `loop` across columns), and Bresenham y-major (1812) / x-major (1850) each in the solid
    `stos` and the `cs:0x161b` XOR form, stepping columns by +-pitch (`inc bp` in the solid x-major loop
    undoes the stos advance).

